From c1875c4c7cc7bb919df5fd5f87bb3fada080bcaf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anthony Ruhier <aruhier@mailbox.org>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 23:26:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: mux: ps883x: don't power the retimer up for the
 safe state

On Qualcomm laptops the retimer gets mode switches from two places:
pmic_glink_altmode, and the Type-C class through the UCSI alternate
modes. The class always passes the alternate mode in the retimer
state, also when moving to the safe state on mode exit, and
ps883x_set() takes any state with an alternate mode as a DisplayPort
configuration.

When a DisplayPort sink is unplugged, pmic_glink_altmode reports the
orientation as none and the safe state, and the retimer is put in
reset. The safe state from the class exiting the DisplayPort mode can
come after that, and it powers the retimer back up and programs it as
connected. Nothing follows, so the retimer stays powered with nothing
plugged until the next connection on that port. On a Lenovo Yoga Slim
7x (X1E80100) that is about 550 mW at idle.

Seen with kprobes on ps883x_sw_set(), ps883x_retimer_set(),
ps883x_enable_vregs() and ps883x_reset() after unplugging a monitor:

  sw_set:   name="5-0008-switch" orient=0
  rtmr_set: name="5-0008-retimer" alt=0x0 mode=0
  reset:    name="5-0008-switch"
  sw_set:   name="5-0008-switch" orient=0
  rtmr_set: name="5-0008-retimer" alt=0xffff000d043dfc08 mode=0
  vregs_on: name="5-0008-switch"

The safe state never needs the retimer powered, so leave it in reset
for that state. Any other mode still powers it up.

Fixes: b38fa168a848 ("usb: typec: mux: ps883x: Power the retimer off when not in use")
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/usb/typec/mux/ps883x.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/typec/mux/ps883x.c b/drivers/usb/typec/mux/ps883x.c
index 64e0a61b776a1..0461718123389 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/typec/mux/ps883x.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/mux/ps883x.c
@@ -196,6 +196,15 @@ static int ps883x_set(struct ps883x_retimer *retimer, struct typec_retimer_state
 	int cfg2 = 0x00;
 	bool reset = false;
 
+	/*
+	 * The safe state never needs the retimer powered. The Type-C class
+	 * sends it with the alternate mode still set when the mode is exited,
+	 * which can happen after a disconnect already put the retimer in
+	 * reset: don't take that as a DP configuration and power it back up.
+	 */
+	if (state->mode == TYPEC_STATE_SAFE && retimer->in_reset)
+		return 0;
+
 	if (retimer->orientation == TYPEC_ORIENTATION_REVERSE)
 		cfg0 |= CONN_STATUS_0_ORIENTATION_REVERSED;
 
